Looking at the full call chain, the issue stems from a null parent pointer in kernfs_remove_by_name_ns when trying to remove the 'typec' entry during partner unregistration.

The problem has two layers: first, sysfs_create_link can fail silently during link creation without recording that the link was never established, yet the unregister path unconditionally tries to remove it anyway. But the real culprit for the "no directory" warning is that port->usb2_dev and port->usb3_dev hold device pointers without proper reference counting, so the device can be removed from sysfs before the typec partner unregistration happens, leaving kobj->sd as NULL when we attempt the removal. I'm recalling specific upstream commits that address the typec partner linking issues, though I need to be careful about verifying exact commit hashes without direct access. The core problem is clear from the code evidence: there's a race condition between device removal and partner unlinking, and the kernel tree already has some protections like the partner_link_lock, but I should acknowledge the limitations in pinpointing the exact upstream fixes.
## Root cause chain (file:line evidence)

### 1. Where the WARN is raised — `parent == NULL`, not "link missing"

`fs/kernfs/dir.c:1724`
```c
int kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name,
			     const struct ns_common *ns)
{
	if (!parent) {                                    /* :1730 */
		WARN(1, KERN_WARNING "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n",
			name);
		return -ENOENT;                           /* :1733 */
	}
	...
	kn = kernfs_find_ns(parent, name, ns);            /* :1739 */
	...
	return kn ? 0 : -ENOENT;                          /* :1748-1751 */
}
```

This is the crucial distinction:

* A **missing link** in an existing directory takes the `kernfs_find_ns()` path (`:1739`) and returns `-ENOENT` **silently** — no WARN.
* The `"no directory"` WARN at `:1731` fires **only when `parent` is NULL**, i.e. `kobj->sd == NULL` — the *kobject the link lives in has already been torn out of sysfs* (or was never added). `sysfs_remove_link()` passes `kobj->sd` straight through as `parent` (`fs/sysfs/symlink.c:143` → `kernfs_remove_by_name`, `include/linux/kernfs.h:638`).

So the message means: **we tried to delete the `typec` symlink from a `struct device` whose sysfs directory no longer exists.**

### 2. The offending function — `typec_partner_unlink_device()`

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906`
```c
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
{
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));   /* :908 */
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");                 /* :909  <-- WARN source */

	if (partner->deattach)
		partner->deattach(partner, dev);
}
```

Line **909** is the exact call that produces `"can not remove 'typec'"` — the name `"typec"` appears nowhere else in the removal paths. `dev` here is the **`struct usb_device`** enumerated behind the connector.

### 3. Its unconditional caller — `typec_unregister_partner()`

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174`
```c
void typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)
{
	...
	port = to_typec_port(partner->dev.parent);        /* :1181 */

	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);             /* :1183 */
	if (port->usb2_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);   /* :1185 */
		port->usb2_dev = NULL;
	}
	if (port->usb3_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);   /* :1189 */
		port->usb3_dev = NULL;
	}

	device_unregister(&partner->dev);                 /* :1193 */
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
}
```

The only gate is *"is the pointer non-NULL"* — there is **no check that the link was ever created, nor that `dev` is still live in sysfs.**

### 4. The two asymmetries that make the pointer stale

**(a) Link creation is fallible and silent; removal is unconditional.**

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:888`
```c
static void typec_partner_link_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
{
	int ret;

	ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
	if (ret)
		return;                                   /* :894  early out, nothing recorded */

	ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	if (ret) {
		sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");
		return;                                   /* :899  early out, nothing recorded */
	}
	...
}
```
`void` return, no state bit. Meanwhile `typec_register_partner()` sets nothing on failure either — it just calls the linker and moves on (`class.c:1158-1161`). So `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` stay populated even when no link exists.

**(b) `port->usb2_dev` / `usb3_dev` are raw, unreferenced `struct device *`.**

They are published in `typec_partner_attach()` (`class.c:2144`):
```c
	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);             /* :2151 */
	if (udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER) {
		usb_mode = USB_MODE_USB2;
		port->usb2_dev = dev;                     /* :2154  no get_device() */
	} else {
		usb_mode = USB_MODE_USB3;
		port->usb3_dev = dev;                     /* :2157  no get_device() */
	}
	partner = typec_get_partner(port);                /* :2160 */
	if (partner) {
		typec_partner_set_usb_mode(partner, usb_mode);
		typec_partner_link_device(partner, dev);  /* :2163 */
		put_device(&partner->dev);
	}
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
```

and are supposed to be cleared by the mirror callback `typec_partner_deattach()` (`class.c:2169`):
```c
	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	partner = typec_get_partner(port);                /* :2175 */
	if (partner) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev);/* :2177 */
		put_device(&partner->dev);
	}
	if (port->usb2_dev == dev)
		port->usb2_dev = NULL;                    /* :2182 */
	else if (port->usb3_dev == dev)
		port->usb3_dev = NULL;                    /* :2184 */
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
```

Both are wired in at `class.c:2752-2753`:
```c
	port->con.attach   = typec_partner_attach;
	port->con.deattach = typec_partner_deattach;
```

The critical detail: `typec_attach()` is called from `usb_new_device()` **only if the port device has a connector** — `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2722`:
```c
		typec_attach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev);
```
and `typec_attach`/`typec_deattach` are no-ops when `con` is NULL (`include/linux/usb/typec.h:404-414`).

**The race:** if the `usb_device` is torn down via a path that does *not* reach `typec_deattach()` (or reaches it after the connector-change work has already started), `device_del()` → `sysfs_remove_dir()` has already set `udev->dev.kobj.sd = NULL`, while `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` still point at that now-desysfs'd device. Note `partner_link_lock` (`class.c:1183`, `:2151`, `:2174`) serialises attach/deattach against unregister, but it does **not** synchronise against USB core's `device_del()`, and no reference is held — so it cannot prevent this.

### 5. How UCSI triggers it at boot

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138`
```c
static void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)
{
	if (!con->partner)
		return;                                   /* :1141  only guard */

	typec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);
	...
	ucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);
	ucsi_unregister_cable(con);
	typec_unregister_partner(con->partner);           /* :1151  <-- enters the chain */
	memset(&con->partner_identity, 0, sizeof(con->partner_identity));
	con->partner = NULL;
}
```

Reached from the workqueue handler that matches your `kworker/6:0` backtrace — `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264`:
```c
static void ucsi_handle_connector_change(struct work_struct *work)
{
	...
	if (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {        /* :1306 */
		...
		if (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {
			ucsi_register_partner(con);        /* :1313 */
			ucsi_partner_task(con, ucsi_check_connection, 1, HZ);  /* :1314 */
			...
		} else {
			ucsi_unregister_partner(con);     /* :1325  <-- your trace */
		}
	}
```

There is a **second, deferred** entry point that makes this reproducible specifically at boot — `ucsi_check_connection()` (`ucsi.c:1201`), queued as a delayed partner task at `:1314`:
```c
	if (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {
		if (prev_state)
			return 0;
		ucsi_register_partner(con);               /* :1215 */
		...
	} else {
		ucsi_partner_change(con);
		ucsi_port_psy_changed(con);
		ucsi_unregister_partner(con);             /* :1221 */
	}
```

On the Dell Pro Precision 5 16, BIOS 0.4.53's PPM reports a spurious connect on the Thunderbolt/USB4 connector during early boot, then a disconnect ~1 s later. The register→(USB enumeration)→unregister sequence collapses into the window where the `usb_device` is being reaped, so `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` is stale by the time `class.c:909` runs. `ucsi_init()` and `ucsi_unregister()` (`ucsi.c:2147`) call the same unregister path, giving additional boot/shutdown windows.

## Is a fix present in this tree?

**No.** This tree has only *partial* hardening:

| Mitigation present | Location | Why insufficient |
|---|---|---|
| `partner_link_lock` serialising attach/deattach vs. unregister | `class.c:1183`, `2151`, `2174`, init at `2746` | Doesn't synchronise against USB core `device_del()`; no refcount held |
| `deattach` clears the pointers | `class.c:2181-2184` | Only runs if `port_dev->connector` is set and only if `typec_deattach()` is actually reached before teardown |
| `IS_ERR_OR_NULL(partner)` guard | `class.c:1178` | Guards the *partner*, not the *usb_device* |
| `if (!con->partner)` guard | `ucsi.c:1140` | Same — guards the partner only |

The missing piece is precisely a validity check on `dev->kobj.sd` (or a `get_device()`/tracked-link-state) before `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` at `class.c:909`.

## Upstream fix

I want to be explicit about the limit of my evidence here: everything above is verified against the on-disk source, but **I cannot confirm a commit hash or `Fixes:` tag from the codegraph index** — it indexes symbols and source, not git history. I won't invent one.

What I can state with confidence from the code:

* The bug is a **benign-but-noisy `WARN_ON`** — `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()` returns `-ENOENT` (`dir.c:1733`) and `sysfs_remove_link()` discards the return value. There is no memory corruption or leak beyond the orphaned symlink; the `WARN` is the entire user-visible impact.
* The regression window is the introduction of `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` partner symlinking plus `partner_link_lock` in `class.c` — that is what any upstream `Fixes:` tag would point at.

To pin down the exact upstream commit/series, run in your tree:

```bash
git log --oneline -- drivers/usb/typec/class.c | head -40
git log -L 888,913:drivers/usb/typec/class.c
git log -L 1174,1196:drivers/usb/typec/class.c
git log --oneline --grep='partner_link_lock'
git log --oneline --grep='usb2_dev'
# and against linux-next / usb-next:
git log --oneline v6.17.. -- drivers/usb/typec/class.c drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c
```
Then check `linux-usb@vger.kernel.org` for threads mentioning `"can not remove 'typec'"`.

## Suggested fix shape

Two complementary changes, both in `drivers/usb/typec/class.c`:

1. **Guard the unlink** — make `typec_partner_unlink_device()` skip a device already gone from sysfs:
```c
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
{
	if (!dev->kobj.sd)          /* already removed from sysfs */
		return;
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");
	...
}
```

2. **Take a reference** — `get_device()` in `typec_partner_attach()` (`class.c:2154`, `:2157`) and matching `put_device()` in `typec_partner_deattach()` (`:2182`, `:2184`) and `typec_unregister_partner()` (`:1186`, `:1190`), so the pointers can never outlive the device.

(1) alone silences the WARN; (2) closes the underlying lifetime hole. As an immediate workaround, the warning is harmless and can be ignored, or suppressed with `sysctl kernel.panic_on_warn=0` (default) — there is no functional Type-C/Thunderbolt impact.
